<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ponna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ponna was a Jain poet of the tenth century at the court of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, who gave him the title Ubhayakavichakravarti, 'emperor among poets in two languages', for his writing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. His best-known work is the Shantipurana, on the life of the sixteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a> he is counted among the 'three gems' of early Kannada literature.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ponna was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet at the court of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, who reigned from 939 to 968 CE. His date is given as about 945. According to the scholars Nilakanta Shastri and E. P. Rice he belonged to Punganur, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, and later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, the Rashtrakuta capital. Krishna III honoured him with the title Ubhaya Kavichakravarthi, emperor among poets, for his command of Kannada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. According to the scholar R. Narasimhacharya he claimed superiority over all the poets of his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known surviving works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> are the Shantipurana, the Bhuvanaika-Ramabhyudaya and the Jinaksharamale. The Shantipurana, in the champu style that mixes prose and verse, is a Jain purana on Shantinatha, the sixteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>. It was written to commemorate the nirvana of a Jain guru, Jainachandra Deva. It has twelve sections, nine on Shantinatha's eleven previous births and three on his life. Ponna borrowed much from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> and seems to have used a narrative poem by a Kannada poet, Asaga, whose works are lost. His claim to surpass Asaga shows that Asaga was thought important.</p><p>The Bhuvanaika-Ramabhyudaya praises a patron. D. L. Narasimhachar first thought it honoured Shankaraganda, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, but Govinda Pai argued in 1936 that Krishna III himself held the title Bhuvanaikarama, and Narasimhachar later accepted this. The Jinaksharamale is an acrostic poem in 39 chapters praising Jain saints and Tirthankars. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> work attributed to him survives in a few stanzas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ponna is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Adikavi Pampa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a> as one of the three gems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature. The classical age of Kannada literature is usually reckoned from the middle of the tenth century for about a hundred and fifty years. These poets set a standard in poetic form that influenced later poets for centuries. Their champu style drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> masters, including the prose of Bana and the verse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>, while native forms such as the three-line tripadi appear in their narratives. Just as Ponna praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, Pampa compared his patron Arikesari to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and Ranna compared Satyashraya to Bhima.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ponna (poet)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…on poetics attributed to Amoghavarsha I or his court poet Srivijaya, is the earliest surviving literary work in Kannada, and in the tenth century the poets Pampa, author of the Vikramarjuna Vijaya and a Jain Adipurana completed in 941, and <b>Ponna</b> worked under the Rashtrakutas and their feudatories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Shaiva sects, including the Kalamukha ascetics who ran large temple colleges at Balligave and elsewhere, enjoyed strong royal patronage; Jainism remained influential, especially among merchants and noblewomen such as Attimabbe, who sponsored copies of <b>Ponna</b>’s works and a Jain temple at Lakkundi; and Vaishnavism grew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
